# Line endings.
#
# The repository stores text as LF and `text=auto` converts on checkout, so a
# Windows working tree gets CRLF (core.autocrlf=true, which Git for Windows sets
# by default) while a Linux or macOS one gets LF. Committed content is identical
# either way, which is the point: the normalisation happens on the way in, so a
# diff between two machines is never a diff about carriage returns.
#
# Without this file the outcome depends on each clone's core.autocrlf setting,
# and files written directly by tools — editors, formatters, generators — keep
# whatever those tools wrote. That is how this tree came to hold 55 LF files
# beside 17 CRLF ones, with Git GUIs reporting "no changes or it's a binary file"
# on diffs whose only content was line endings.
* text=auto

# Binary content must never be converted: EOL translation corrupts it. The
# README screenshots are the live case — a PNG that has had its 0x0D bytes
# rewritten is simply a broken PNG.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.ttf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.vsix binary
